<Article
  title="Without a registered will in the UAE: what the default rules actually say"
  description="Frozen accounts, a fixed distribution formula, property that cannot be sold, and a court-chosen guardian. What UAE inheritance law actually says when there is no registered will."
  image="/images/article-signing.jpg"
  minutes={6}
>
  <p>
    Most expats in the UAE have never read what happens to their assets if
    something happens to them without a registered will. Not because the
    information is hidden — it is published by the government — but because
    nobody enjoys looking at it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide walks through the defaults, calmly and precisely. None of this
    is a flaw in the system: the institutions here do their job well. The
    system simply works on <strong>its</strong> assumptions when it cannot find
    <strong>yours</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Your bank accounts could be frozen</h2>
  <p>
    When a UAE bank is notified of a death, it could freeze the associated
    accounts. <strong>Joint accounts too.</strong> The freeze could last weeks
    or months while the courts process the estate.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a surviving spouse, that can mean the money for rent, school fees and
    daily expenses sits behind glass — visible, untouchable — at precisely the
    wrong moment.
  </p>

  <h2>A fixed formula decides who receives what</h2>
  <p>
    Without a will, UAE inheritance law applies a default distribution. For
    non-Muslims, the government's own FAQ states it plainly:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    “The surviving spouse shall receive half of the inheritance and the other
    half shall be distributed equally between the children of the deceased (no
    difference between males and females).”
    <cite>— ADJD official FAQ, based on Article 11 (2) of Law No. 14 of 2021</cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    It is a fixed formula. It might match what you want. It might not. The
    point is that <strong>you do not get a say in it</strong> — the formula
    decides, not you.
  </p>

  <h2>Property cannot move until the court does</h2>
  <p>
    Property cannot be sold and typically cannot be rented out until the court
    completes the transfer. Your family keeps the asset on paper — without
    being able to use it, sell it, or draw income from it in the meantime.
  </p>

  <h2>The court chooses who looks after your children</h2>
  <p>
    Without a will naming a guardian, UAE courts decide who takes care of your
    children until they reach the age of majority. Nobody will know who
    <strong>you</strong> would have wanted — because it was never written down.
  </p>

  <h2>If you are not legally married</h2>
  <p>
    Under UAE law, an unmarried partner has <strong>no automatic legal
    right</strong> to your estate. It does not matter how long you have been
    together. If you want your partner protected, it has to be written — a will
    is the instrument that does it.
  </p>

  <h2>The point is not fear. It is choice.</h2>
  <p>
    Every default above exists because the system needs an answer when yours is
    missing. A registered UAE will replaces each default with your own
    instruction: who receives what, who manages the process, who looks after
    your children.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the process is far simpler than most people expect — 2 to 3 hours of
    your time, done remotely, with a specialist team coordinating every step.
  </p>
</Article>
